  "answer": "Teriflunomide-induced differentiation in uridine-salvage-poor AML through DHODH inhibition\n\nHypothesis. In differentiation-arrested, non-APL AML with prospectively demonstrated low uridine-salvage capacity, teriflunomide will inhibit DHODH, reduce pyrimidine availability, and induce myeloid differentiation before substantial apoptosis, subsequently reducing leukemia-propagating activity. This effect will occur within independently verified clinically attainable unbound exposure limits and spare normal hematopoietic progenitors sufficiently to provide a preclinical therapeutic window.\n\nSupported premises. DHODH inhibition promoted differentiation and affected disease-related endpoints in experimental AML systems [S4]. This supports investigating the pathway, but does not establish teriflunomide efficacy, achievable exposure, or selectivity. Metabolic vulnerability also need not imply one universal AML compartment: S1 identified oxidative-phosphorylation dependence in studied quiescent leukemia stem cells, whereas cytarabine-resistant cells in S2 were not consistently enriched for that population [S1, S2]. Accordingly, differentiation, bulk-cell survival, and leukemia-propagating function must be measured separately. Venetoclax–azacitidine supplies an established metabolic comparator, not the proposed repurposing discovery [S3].\n\nUnverified prior knowledge requiring confirmation. Teriflunomide is approved for relapsing multiple sclerosis and inhibits DHODH; DHODH catalyzes dihydroorotate-to-orotate conversion in de novo pyrimidine synthesis; uridine salvage can bypass this pathway. These drug-approval and biochemical recollections are not verified by the packet. The speculative bridges are that teriflunomide can reproduce the differentiation phenotype reported for DHODH inhibition, that weak salvage predicts susceptibility under marrow-relevant conditions, and that normal progenitors retain sufficient metabolic flexibility to tolerate the same exposure. The connection between nucleotide depletion and release of differentiation arrest remains mechanistically unresolved here.\n\nAlternative explanations. Apparent maturation could reflect preferential death of immature blasts rather than differentiation of surviving leukemic cells. Reduced colony formation could represent reversible growth arrest, nonspecific metabolic stress, or off-target toxicity rather than loss of leukemia-propagating capacity. Uridine availability or stromal support might also eliminate an effect observed in artificial culture conditions.\n\nDiscriminating preclinical strategy. First verify approval, pharmacology, safety constraints, and human unbound exposure ranges independently. Enroll fresh primary AML specimens across pretreatment low- and high-salvage strata, alongside healthy-donor hematopoietic progenitors. Define strata through isotope-traced uridine utilization before drug testing, using a prespecified extracellular uridine range rather than uridine-free medium. Test exposure-matched teriflunomide in protein-containing cultures, including stromal coculture, with vehicle, an independently validated DHODH inhibitor, inducible DHODH suppression, and venetoclax–azacitidine comparators [S3, S4].\n\nMeasure biochemical DHODH inhibition and early changes in dihydroorotate, orotate, and pyrimidine pools before assessing viability. Test uridine rescue, interpreting a negative rescue only when uptake and nucleotide restoration are demonstrated. Concordant effects from genetic DHODH suppression, together with metabolite restoration and reversal of the phenotype by rescue, would support pathway dependence. Track viable leukemic-cell descendants using morphology, prespecified maturation markers, and cell-cycle/apoptosis measurements to distinguish differentiation from selective killing. After washout, assess serial replating and limiting-dilution leukemia initiation using equal viable-cell inputs; surface-marker changes alone will not establish stem-cell depletion. Compare normal progenitor colony formation at identical unbound exposures. Advance responsive specimens to patient-derived xenografts only after establishing exposure feasibility, measuring marrow drug levels, pharmacodynamic engagement, leukemia initiation after treatment, and hematopoietic toxicity.\n\nFalsification and limitations. The central causal chain is falsified if exposure-bounded treatment demonstrably engages DHODH and depletes pyrimidines in prospectively classified low-salvage AML, yet fails to induce genuine differentiation or reduce leukemia-propagating activity after washout. Activity restricted to supra-achievable exposure, or comparable impairment of normal progenitors, rejects the proposed therapeutic window even if the pathway mechanism operates. Substantial phenotype persistence after confirmed nucleotide-restoring rescue would argue against the proposed mediation. Donor heterogeneity, marrow nutrient availability, stromal rescue, and species-dependent pharmacokinetics limit interpretation. Normal colony preservation cannot establish long-term hematopoietic safety, and approval for multiple sclerosis does not establish suitability for AML.",
